{
  "teamId": 115,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Colorado Rockies arrived with cautious optimism and immediately stumbled, splitting their opening weekend before a rocky April that left them barely treading water at 14-13. The bullpen showed early cracks — Bryan Shaw surrendering a three-run bomb to Hunter Renfroe in April set an ominous tone — but Ian Desmond's timely blasts and a resilient offense kept the Rockies from falling too far behind. May and June told a story of a team fighting itself as much as its opponents: CarGo and Iannetta provided sparks, but the relief corps continued to leak runs, and a brutal 11-16 June raised real questions about whether this group had the mettle for a playoff run.\n\nThen came July, and everything changed. The Rockies went a scorching 17-6, the best month in the franchise's recent memory, carried by an offense that was finally clicking from top to bottom. Trevor Story's 20th home run, Raimel Tapia's grand slam, Nolan Arenado's own bases-clearing blast, and Charlie Blackmon's continued production turned Coors Field into a fortress. The team had found its identity as a slugging, never-quit collective, and even when the bullpen stumbled — Wade Davis in particular had a nightmarish stretch — the lineup simply kept scoring.\n\nAugust was a month of extremes, perfectly embodied by Ryan McMahon. The young first baseman delivered back-to-back heroic performances, including a three-run walk-off homer on August 11th that registered as the season's highest single positive play, while Wade Davis authored an ugly three-homer blow in Milwaukee that nearly cost Colorado its momentum. Ian Desmond's two-run shot on August 23rd and DJ LeMahieu's grand slam on August 27th steadied the ship, and a September surge — 19-9 — carried the Rockies to 91 wins and a Wild Card berth, the franchise's best finish in years.\n\nOctober arrived with electric promise. In the Wild Card game against the Cubs, Tony Wolters delivered a clutch single to score Trevor Story and briefly ignite hope, but Adam Ottavino's fateful double to Javier Báez dampened the moment and Colorado survived to advance. The NLDS against Milwaukee, however, proved to be a bridge too far. In a punishing finish, Ottavino surrendered a Mike Moustakas single that plated the decisive run, and the dream of a pennant dissolved. Still, 91 wins and a postseason appearance represented a genuine breakthrough for a Rockies team that proved, for one glorious summer, they belonged among the NL's elite.",
